*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* {{NAME}} - shared program body
*
* This is the file to edit. It contains the whole program and nothing
* loader-specific: no AORG, no cartridge header, no DEF. Each file in
* targets/ supplies the prologue its loader needs and then COPYs this.
*
* That is what lets one source ship as a cartridge, as an
* Editor/Assembler program, and on an Extended BASIC disk, without
* keeping three copies of the code in step.
*
* MAIN is the entry point. Everything below it is yours.
*----------------------------------------------------------------------

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Hardware
*
* The TMS9918 video processor is reached through two memory-mapped
* ports. To write to VDP address A, send the low byte of A, then the
* high byte with bit 6 set (>40), then the data. To read, send the low
* byte and the high byte with bit 6 clear, then read.
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
VDPWD  EQU  >8C00             * VDP write data
VDPWA  EQU  >8C02             * VDP set read/write address
VDPRD  EQU  >8800             * VDP read data
VDPSTA EQU  >8802             * VDP status

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Scratchpad RAM, >8300 to >83FF
*
* 256 bytes of fast RAM inside the console, and the only RAM a bare
* console has. On a cartridge the code itself is in ROM and cannot be
* written, so anything that changes at run time lives here or in VDP
* RAM. Keep that in mind before adding a variable next to the code.
*
* >8300..>831F is our workspace: the sixteen registers R0..R15.
* >8320 upwards is free for variables. The console keeps its own state
* from >83C0 up, so do not go past >83BF.
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
WRKSP  EQU  >8300             * Our workspace - R0..R15 live here
R0LB   EQU  WRKSP+1           * Low byte of R0, handy for VDP addresses

COUNT  EQU  >8320             * Example variable. Add your own below.

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* VDP memory map
*
* These must match the register values in REGLD at the bottom.
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
NAMETB EQU  >0000             * Screen image, 24 rows x 32 columns
COLRTB EQU  >0380             * Colour table
PTRNTB EQU  >0800             * Character patterns

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* MAIN - entry point
*
* Every target reaches this label, whatever route it took to get here.
* Interrupts are off and the workspace is ours.
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
MAIN   LIMI 0                 * Interrupts off while we set up
       LWPI WRKSP             * Our own workspace

       CLR  R0
       MOVB R0,@>837A         * No sprites in automatic motion

       BL   @VIDEO            * Set up the screen
       BL   @CLS              * Clear it

* Write the greeting at row 10, column 10.
       LI   R0,10*32+10       * Screen position, as row*32+column
       LI   R1,MSG
       LI   R2,MSGL
       BL   @VMBW

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Your program goes here.
*
* Useful things to know when adding to it:
*
*   - BL @ROUTINE calls; RT returns. R11 holds the return address, so
*     save it before calling something else from inside a routine.
*   - The utilities below need no Editor/Assembler, which is why this
*     runs on a bare console from a cartridge.
*   - Screen position is row*32+column, rows and columns counted from
*     zero, 24 rows of 32 columns.
*   - A character is a byte. Space is >20. The pattern table holds the
*     shapes; write to it with VMBW to define your own.
*   - LIMI 2 enables interrupts, which you need for sound and for
*     automatic sprite motion. LIMI 0 turns them off again.
*----------------------------------------------------------------------

HALT   JMP  HALT              * Spin forever. Replace with your loop.

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Utilities
*
* Written out here rather than REFed from the Editor/Assembler, so the
* program does not depend on that cartridge being present.
*----------------------------------------------------------------------

* Each of these is a leaf: it calls nothing else, so R11 holds the
* return address for the whole routine and there is no need to save it.
* Keep it that way, or save R11 before calling out of one of them.

* VIDEO - load the eight VDP registers from REGLD.
*         Clobbers R1, R2, R3.
VIDEO  LI   R1,REGLD
       LI   R2,>8000          * Register 0, write bit set
VIDLP  MOVB *R1+,R3
       MOVB R3,@VDPWA         * Value first
       MOVB R2,@VDPWA         * Then the register number
       AI   R2,>0100
       CI   R2,>8800
       JL   VIDLP
       RT

* CLS - fill the screen with spaces.
*       Clobbers R0, R1, R2.
CLS    LI   R0,NAMETB
       ORI  R0,>4000          * Set the write bit
       SWPB R0
       MOVB R0,@VDPWA         * Low byte of the address first
       SWPB R0
       MOVB R0,@VDPWA         * Then the high byte
       LI   R1,>2000          * Space, in the high byte
       LI   R2,24*32
CLSLP  MOVB R1,@VDPWD
       DEC  R2
       JNE  CLSLP
       RT

* VSBW - write the single byte in the high half of R1 to VDP address R0.
*        Clobbers R0.
VSBW   ORI  R0,>4000
       SWPB R0
       MOVB R0,@VDPWA
       SWPB R0
       MOVB R0,@VDPWA
       MOVB R1,@VDPWD
       RT

* VMBW - write R2 bytes from CPU address R1 to VDP address R0.
*        Clobbers R0, R1, R2.
VMBW   ORI  R0,>4000
       SWPB R0
       MOVB R0,@VDPWA
       SWPB R0
       MOVB R0,@VDPWA
VMBWLP MOVB *R1+,@VDPWD
       DEC  R2
       JNE  VMBWLP
       RT

* VWTR - write the byte in the high half of R1 to VDP register R0.
*        Clobbers R0, R1.
VWTR   SWPB R1
       MOVB R1,@VDPWA
       ORI  R0,>8000
       SWPB R0
       MOVB R0,@VDPWA
       SWPB R0
       RT

*----------------------------------------------------------------------
* Data
*
* On a cartridge this is in ROM and cannot be written. Variables belong
* in scratchpad, next to COUNT above.
*----------------------------------------------------------------------
REGLD  BYTE >00               * R0: graphics I, no external video
       BYTE >E0               * R1: 16K, display on, interrupts on
       BYTE >00               * R2: screen image at >0000
       BYTE >0E               * R3: colour table at >0380
       BYTE >01               * R4: pattern table at >0800
       BYTE >06               * R5: sprite attributes at >0300
       BYTE >00               * R6: sprite patterns at >0000
       BYTE >07               * R7: white on transparent
       EVEN

MSG    TEXT 'HELLO WORLD!'
MSGL   EQU  $-MSG
       EVEN

       END  MAIN
